'''Xasan Garaad''' (born 15 March 1961) is a Burawi politician and trade unionist who has served as the [[Leader of the Socialist Party of Burawa]] since 2018. He has represented [[Samara-9]] in the [[Lower House of Burawa]] since 1994, making him one of the longest-serving members of the legislature. A member of the Socialist Party for over three decades, Garaad is regarded as a moderate within the party, positioned between the progressive wing that defected to the [[Forward Party of Burawa]] in 2014 and the traditionalist establishment.

Garaad contested the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]], placing first in first-preference votes with 30 per cent — the highest share of any candidate — but was defeated in the ranked-choice redistribution by [[Hancil Thea]] of the [[National Party of Burawa]]. He is the Socialist Party candidate in the [[2026 Burawi snap election|2026 snap leadership election]].

== Early life and education ==

Xasan Garaad was born on 15 March 1961 in [[Samara]], the capital city of Burawa. His father, '''Cabdullahi Garaad''', was a steelworker at the [[Burawa Steel Corporation]]; his mother, '''Fadumo Mahadala''', was a homemaker. He was raised in a working-class neighbourhood of Samara in a household connected to the industrial labour movement.

Garaad completed his secondary education in Samara before enrolling at the [[University of Samara]], where he studied political science. His education coincided with the latter years of the Socialist Party's consolidation of power following the [[1962 Crisis]].

=== Kankadadka Metro Bombing ===

{{main|Kankadadka Metro Bombing}}

In 1989, while working as a trade union organiser, Garaad was present in [[Kankadadka]] during the '''[[Kankadadka Metro Bombing]]''', an attack perpetrated by the [[Kwalib Independence Movement]]. He was among the survivors of the bombing, which killed multiple civilians and wounded dozens. The experience reinforced his commitment to public service and shaped his later views on regional security and the Kwalib conflict.

== Early career ==

After graduating from university, Garaad began working as a trade union organiser for steelworkers at the Burawa Steel Corporation. The position brought him into direct contact with the [[Socialist Party of Burawa]], which maintained close ties to organised labour through its decades of governance. His work as an organiser focused on wage negotiations, workplace safety, and collective bargaining.

Garaad's trade union work established his reputation within the Socialist Party as a reliable advocate for labour interests. He was recruited to stand for parliamentary office in the 1994 election.

== Parliamentary career ==

=== 1994 election ===

At age 33, Garaad contested the [[Samara-9]] legislative district in the [[1994 Burawi Lower House election]], winning his first term in the [[Lower House of Burawa]]. The election occurred during the height of the Socialist Party's dominance — the party had held the [[Leadership of the Burawi Republic|Leadership]] continuously since 1990 and controlled the Lower House through a combination of direct party membership and allied progressive coalition partners.

Garaad's victory in Samara-9, a working-class district near the steelworks, reflected his base of support among industrial workers.

=== Early legislative work (1994–1997) ===

In his first term, Garaad authored the [[Industrial Worker Healthcare Reform Act of 1994]], which expanded the legal definition of work-related injuries to include later-in-life cancer diagnoses linked to industrial work. The legislation represented a significant expansion of healthcare protections for manufacturing workers and drew on Garaad's experience as a trade union organiser.

=== Junior minister and technologist leadership (1997–1999) ===

In 1997, Garaad was appointed '''junior minister of innovation''' in the Socialist government. The position brought him into the emerging policy field of technology modernisation, which would become central to his political identity.

In 1998, Garaad was one of the principal authors of the [[Millennium Technology Readiness Act]], which established software and hardware modernisation standards for Burawi government systems in preparation for the year 2000. The legislation sought to address compatibility issues and ensure continuity of government services.

In 1999, Garaad emerged as the leader of the '''"technologist" branch''' of the Socialist Party's progressive wing. He advocated for the digitalisation of Burawi government systems and helped author the [[Burawa.bu Act]], which created a unified online portal for government services. The initiative represented one of the earliest large-scale e-government projects in Burawi history.

=== Burawi National ID program (2008) ===

In 2008, as a regular member within the Ministry of Innovation, Garaad helped spearhead the next-generation [[Burawi National ID]] programme, which introduced {{W|Near-field communication|NFC technology}} to Burawi identity cards. The modernised identification system enabled electronic authentication and streamlined access to government services.

=== 2005 re-election ===

{{main|2005 Burawi Lower House election}}

In the [[2005 Burawi Lower House election]], Garaad nearly lost his Samara-9 seat, winning by a margin of 51 per cent to 49 per cent. The close race reflected voter fatigue with the Socialist Party's long incumbency and growing competition from emerging opposition parties.

=== Vice-chairmanship and Minister of Innovation (2009–2015) ===

From 2009 to 2015, Garaad served as '''vice-chairman of the Socialist Party''', holding a senior position within the party's leadership structure during Cabadlei Carab's tenure as Leader.

From 2011 to 2015, Garaad served as the '''minister of innovation''' in the Socialist government. His tenure coincided with the government's response to the [[Great Recession (Adonia)|Great Recession]], during which he advocated for technology investment as a pathway to economic recovery.

Garaad spearheaded the creation of a Burawi government investment fund to support software startups, including the early-stage investment in [[Sare (company)|Sare]], which later became one of Burawi's largest technology companies.

In 2012, Garaad launched the '''Technology in Education Initiative''', a programme to provide notebook computers to secondary school classrooms to support technology-assisted learning. The initiative sought to address the digital divide and prepare Burawi students for the technology-driven economy.

In 2013, Garaad collaborated with the Ministry of Education to introduce '''programming as a mandatory subject''' in secondary education. He also launched the [[Technology Next Generation Programme]], which brought technology literacy courses to primary education. The combined reforms represented a comprehensive integration of technology education across Burawi's school system.

=== The Great Recession and wage freezes ===

{{main|Great Recession (Adonia)}}

The [[Great Recession (Adonia)|Great Recession]] of 2009 significantly affected Burawa's manufacturing and export-based economy. The Socialist government, led by [[Cabadlei Carab]], instituted an emergency reduction in public sector wage growth, freezing wages at zero per cent in 2009 and raising them only incrementally over the following years (0.25% in 2010, 0.5% in 2011, 0.65% in 2012, 0.55% in 2013, 1.5% in 2014, and 2.5% in 2015). The wage stagnation persisted for nearly eight years.

Garaad remained loyal to the Socialist Party during this period, defending the government's response to the crisis as necessary to preserve macroeconomic stability. However, public frustration with stagnant purchasing power contributed to the erosion of Socialist Party support and enabled [[Marik Sahamin]]'s victory in the [[2015 Burawi leadership election]].

=== 2015 election and party crisis ===

{{main|2015 Burawi leadership election}}

The 2015 election proved catastrophic for the Socialist Party. [[Marik Sahamin]], who had broken from the party's progressive wing in 2014 to found the [[Forward Party of Burawa]], won the Leadership in a triple election. The Socialist Party lost not only the Leadership but also significant parliamentary representation as progressive voters defected to Forward.

Garaad, who had positioned himself as a moderate within the party, survived the electoral collapse and retained his Samara-9 seat. In the aftermath of the defeat, the Socialist Party underwent a leadership transition, with the long-serving Cabadlei Carab stepping down as party leader.

== Leadership of the Socialist Party ==

In 2018, Garaad was elected [[Leader of the Socialist Party of Burawa]], succeeding Cabadlei Carab. His election represented a choice for a moderate figure who could maintain the party's traditional base while attempting to recover voters who had defected to Forward.

As party leader, Garaad emphasised public sector wage reform, traditional socialist governance, and opposition to what he characterised as the excesses of the Sahamin administration. He sought to position the Socialist Party as a stable alternative to both the reformist Forward Party and the conservative National Party.

=== 2025 leadership election ===

{{main|2025 Burawi leadership election}}

Garaad contested the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]] as the Socialist Party candidate, the first leadership election conducted under [[ranked-choice voting]]. He placed first in first-preference votes with 30 per cent — the highest share of any candidate — ahead of [[Hancil Thea]] (28 per cent) and [[Marik Sahamin]] (24 per cent).

However, under the RCV redistribution, Thea accumulated sufficient second-preference support to surpass Garaad and win the election. The result demonstrated the limits of the Socialist Party's base: while Garaad could mobilise the party's traditional supporters, he failed to attract second-preference votes from other parties, whose voters preferred Thea as a geographic and ideological outsider.

== 2026 snap election campaign ==

{{main|2026 Burawi snap election}}

On 22 March 2026, the Socialist Party confirmed that Garaad would serve as its candidate in the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|snap leadership election]] following the recall of [[Hancil Thea]]. The party's platform emphasised public sector wage reform, opposition to Thea's rural-focused policies, and the need for metropolitan leadership after the National Party's brief tenure.

Garaad participated in televised debates on 8 April and 12 April 2026. In the second debate, he engaged in heated exchanges with Thea over the recall's legitimacy, arguing that the Industrial Party agreement demonstrated Thea's unfitness for office.

Opinion polls showed Garaad's support declining over the course of the campaign: 22 per cent on 7 April (leading the field), 21 per cent on 9 April (after the first debate, trailing Muna Xasan's surge to 35 per cent), and 16 per cent on 12 April (trailing Xasan and Thea).

== Political positions ==

Garaad is regarded as a moderate within the Socialist Party, positioned between the progressive wing that defected to Forward in 2014 and the traditionalist establishment that preceded him. His political positions reflect the Socialist Party's historical emphasis on public sector employment, organised labour, and state management of the economy.

On economic policy, Garaad advocates for public sector wage growth, state control of essential industries, and union rights. His background in trade union organising at Burawa Steel informs his close ties to the industrial labour movement. He opposed the Sahamin administration's technology-focused economic shift as neglectful of Burawa's manufacturing base.

On social policy, Garaad is regarded as a moderate, supporting the Socialist Party's traditional positions without the active social liberalism associated with the Forward Party. He supported same-sex marriage recognition in 2016 but has not made social issues central to his political identity.

On regional policy, Garaad has emphasised metropolitan investment and urban governance, positioning himself in opposition to Thea's rural-focused agenda. He has argued that the Dhulsarren sanctions following the HSR Scandal were proportionate and necessary measures against corruption.

On governance, Garaad has positioned himself as a champion of institutional stability, criticising both the Industrial Party agreement and the recall process as examples of political dysfunction.

== Personal life ==

Garaad married '''[[Fartuun Cilmi]]''' in 1992. The couple have three children — two sons and a daughter — and reside in [[Samara]].

